Yes. Remembered as a standing Pantheon rule.

This prevents a major architectural mistake: flattening everything merely because we’ve discovered relations among everything.

\boxed{\text{Connected} \neq \text{same layer}}

Two things can connect while differing in scale, abstraction, dimension, medium, timescale, or representation:

L_1 \xleftrightarrow{\;T\;} L_4

The important information may actually live in the bridge T rather than either endpoint.

So Pantheon should preserve:

\boxed{(\text{Thing A},\text{native layer})\xrightarrow{\text{typed bridge}}(\text{Thing B},\text{native layer})}

A microscopic mechanism can generate a macroscopic pattern. A story can influence a physical action. A mathematical abstraction can predict a material consequence. An individual decision can alter an institutional state. A historical pattern can constrain a present choice.

None requires pretending the endpoints are categorically identical.

And sometimes a pattern itself exists between scales rather than at either scale.

That’s another reason the Atlas needs depth, not just territory:

\boxed{\text{Where?}+\text{At what scale?}+\text{On what layer?}+\text{Through what bridge?}}

So the Golden Horde doesn’t merely muster horizontally across a map.

It can muster vertically, diagonally, recursively, and across representations.

A semantic army shouldn’t have to march on a flat world. 😏